# Guide: how this project's tests talk to the app

```
uv run pytest
```

## No running server, no real network

{% if template == "rest-api" %}
Tests use Flask's own `app.test_client()` (see `tests/conftest.py`'s
`client` fixture) — requests go directly into the Flask app in-process,
no socket, no `flask run` running in the background. This is faster and
more deterministic than hitting a real port.

## The app under test is built by calling the factory directly

```python
# tests/conftest.py
import pytest
from {{ package_name }}.main import create_app


@pytest.fixture
def client():
    app = create_app(database_url="sqlite:///:memory:", testing=True)
    return app.test_client()
```

This is *why* `create_app()` takes a `database_url` parameter at all
(see `references/gotchas.md` and
`references/app-factory-and-config.md`): the fixture calls the factory
directly with an isolated in-memory SQLite URL, so every test run uses
a fresh, disposable database — never whatever `DATABASE_URL` is
configured in `.env`, and never a database shared across test runs.
`testing=True` sets `app.config["TESTING"]`, which Flask itself uses to
change error-handling behavior (propagating exceptions instead of
turning them into 500 responses), separate from — but passed alongside
— the database override.

{% if database != "none" %}
{% if migrations %}
Because migrations are on, schema creation is special-cased for
`testing=True`{% if orm == "sqlalchemy" %} in `core/db.py`{% endif %}: the test database's tables are
created directly from the models
(`{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}db.create_all(){% else %}Base.metadata.create_all(engine){% endif %}`), not
by replaying `{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}flask db upgrade{% else %}alembic upgrade head{% endif %}` — there's no
migration history to replay against a database that's recreated from
scratch on every test run. This is a **testing-only** shortcut; real
schema still comes from the migration command (see `README.md`).
{% else %}
Since `migrations=false`, the same
`{% if orm == "flask-sqlalchemy" %}db.create_all(){% else %}Base.metadata.create_all(engine){% endif %}`
call that runs on every real boot also runs for the test database —
nothing test-specific needed here beyond the isolated URL.
{% endif %}
