## Adding a test for a new endpoint

Mirror `tests/test_main.py`: a `def test_...(client)` function using
the `client` fixture, asserting on `response.status_code` and
`response.get_json()`. One test per behavior (create, list, get-by-id,
404-when-missing, delete) rather than one large test asserting
everything — a failure then points at exactly what broke.

```python
def test_item_crud(client) -> None:
    create_response = client.post("/items/", json={"name": "Widget"})
    assert create_response.status_code == 201
    item = create_response.get_json()

    get_response = client.get(f"/items/{item['id']}")
    assert get_response.status_code == 200

    delete_response = client.delete(f"/items/{item['id']}")
    assert delete_response.status_code == 204
```

{% else %}
Tests use Flask's own `app.test_client()`, built directly from the
module-level `app` in `main.py` — requests go directly into the app
in-process, no socket, no `flask run` running in the background:

```python
# tests/test_main.py
from {{ package_name }}.main import app


def test_read_root() -> None:
    client = app.test_client()
    response = client.get("/")
    assert response.status_code == 200
    assert response.json == {"message": "Hello, World!"}
```

There's no fixture here because there's no per-test setup needed (no
database, no config to override) — `client = app.test_client()` at the
top of each test is enough. If you add anything a test needs to
isolate (config, a database), that's the point at which to introduce a
`tests/conftest.py` fixture and, likely, switch `main.py` to the
`rest-api` template's `create_app()` factory shape (see
`references/gotchas.md`) so tests can build an isolated app instead of
importing the one real one.

## Adding a test for a new route

Mirror `tests/test_main.py`: a `def test_...()` function that builds
its own `client = app.test_client()` and asserts on
`response.status_code`/`response.json`.
